          "id": 5,
          "title": "Major Schools of Western Psychology",
          "explanation": "Create a timeline of major schools and movements. For each, learn its central question, subject matter, method/approach and distinctive contribution.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nCreate a timeline of major schools and movements. For each, learn its central question, subject matter, method/approach and distinctive contribution.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nStructuralism • Functionalism • Psychoanalytical psychology • Gestalt psychology • Behaviorism • Humanistic-Existential psychology • Transpersonal psychology • Cognitive revolution • Multiculturalism\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Structuralism”?\nA. Gestalt psychology\nB. Structuralism\nC. Functionalism\nD. Psychoanalytical psychology\nAnswer: Structuralism\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Functionalism”?\nA. Structuralism\nB. Psychoanalytical psychology\nC. Gestalt psychology\nD. Functionalism\nAnswer: Functionalism\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Structuralism”?\nA. Psychoanalytical psychology\nB. Gestalt psychology\nC. Structuralism\nD. Functionalism\nAnswer: Structuralism\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Functionalism”?\nA. Functionalism\nB. Structuralism\nC. Psychoanalytical psychology\nD. Gestalt psychology\nAnswer: Functionalism\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Psychoanalytical psychology”?\nA. Structuralism\nB. Functionalism\nC. Gestalt psychology\nD. Psychoanalytical psychology\nAnswer: Psychoanalytical psychology\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 1: Foundations of Psychology\nQ1. The primary subject matter of psychology is\na. the philosophical concept of the psyche\nb. the behavior of organisms\nc. the conscious mind\nd. the unconscious mind\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 1: Foundations of Psychology\nQ2. Which one of the following is not a goal of scientific psychology?\na. To abstract behavior\nb. To explain behavior\nc. To predict behavior\nd. To control behavior\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 1: Foundations of Psychology\nQ3. What characterizes a school of psychology?\na. Its physiological research\nb. Its stand on Gestalt psychology\nc. Its orientation toward psychoanalysis\nd. Its viewpoint and assumptions\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 1: Foundations of Psychology\nQ4. Functionalism, associated with William James, is particularly interested in\na. introspection\nb. the structure of consciousness\nc. how the mind works\nd. developmental psychology\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 1: Foundations of Psychology\nQ5. Which one of the following is correctly associated with the German word Gestalt?\na. Neuron\nb. Organized whole\nc. Physiological psychology\nd. Repression\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 1: Foundations of Psychology\nQ6. What school of psychology indicates that it is important to study behavior itself, not the mind or consciousness?\na. Behaviorism\nb. Structuralism\nc. Psychoanalysis\nd. Functionalism\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 1: Foundations of Psychology\nQ7. The principal assumption of psychoanalysis is that\na. habits determine behavior\nb. human beings do not have an unconscious mental life\nc. human beings have an unconscious mental life\nd. all motives are inborn\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 1: Foundations of Psychology\nQ8. The cognitive viewpoint stresses the importance of\na. learning\nb. thinking\nc. motivation\nd. biological drives\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 1: Foundations of Psychology\nQ9. What viewpoint stresses the importance of the activity of the brain and nervous system?\na. The psychodynamic viewpoint\nb. The learning viewpoint\nc. The humanistic viewpoint\nd. The biological viewpoint\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 1: Foundations of Psychology\nQ10. Psychotherapy is a work activity associated with what field of psychology?\na. Experimental psychology\nb. Developmental psychology\nc. Clinical psychology\nd. Physiological psychology\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Structuralism?\nA: analysis of the structure of conscious experience\nQ: Key points for Structuralism\nA: introspection; elements of consciousness; Titchener\nQ: What is Functionalism?\nA: study of what mental processes and behavior do, especially their adaptive functions\nQ: Key points for Functionalism\nA: William James; adaptation; stream of consciousness\nQ: What is Psychoanalytical psychology?\nA: unconscious processes, conflict and dynamic personality structure\nQ: Key points for Psychoanalytical psychology\nA: Freud; unconscious; psychosexual development; defense mechanisms\nQ: What is Gestalt psychology?\nA: organized wholes and principles of perceptual organization\nQ: Key points for Gestalt psychology\nA: Wertheimer, Köhler, Koffka; figure-ground; grouping laws; insight",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Create a timeline of major schools and movements. For each, learn its central question, subject matter, method/approach and distinctive contribution.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nFoundations of Psychology: The book introduces psychology as the scientific study of behavior and mental life, then traces major historical schools and viewpoints. It presents the goals of scientific psychology—description, explanation, prediction and control—and discusses structuralism, functionalism, Gestalt psychology, psychoanalysis and behaviorism alongside biological, learning, cognitive, sociocultural and humanistic viewpoints. It also distinguishes major fields such as clinical, counseling, developmental, experimental and physiological psychology.",
