          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Ontology”?\nA. Ontology\nB. Epistemology\nC. Methodology\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Ontology\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Epistemology”?\nA. Methodology\nB. None of the above\nC. Epistemology\nD. Ontology\nAnswer: Epistemology\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Ontology”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Ontology\nC. Epistemology\nD. Methodology\nAnswer: Ontology\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Epistemology”?\nA. Ontology\nB. Methodology\nC. None of the above\nD. Epistemology\nAnswer: Epistemology\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Methodology”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Methodology\nC. Ontology\nD. Epistemology\nAnswer: Methodology\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Ontology?\nA: assumptions about what exists or the nature of reality\nQ: Key points for Ontology\nA: being; reality; objects of inquiry\nQ: What is Epistemology?\nA: how knowledge is produced, justified and known\nQ: Key points for Epistemology\nA: sources of knowledge; justification; knower-known relationship\nQ: What is Methodology?\nA: the overall logic and rationale for investigating a research question\nQ: Key points for Methodology\nA: research strategy; paradigm-method link; design choices",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Ontology asks what is assumed to exist; epistemology asks how knowledge is possible or justified; methodology concerns how inquiry is conducted. Learn the distinction and their relationship within a paradigm.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nFoundations of Psychology: The book introduces psychology as the scientific study of behavior and mental life, then traces major historical schools and viewpoints. It presents the goals of scientific psychology—description, explanation, prediction and control—and discusses structuralism, functionalism, Gestalt psychology, psychoanalysis and behaviorism alongside biological, learning, cognitive, sociocultural and humanistic viewpoints. It also distinguishes major fields such as clinical, counseling, developmental, experimental and physiological psychology.",
