          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Positivism”?\nA. Positivism\nB. Post-Positivism\nC. Critical perspective\nD. Social Constructionism\nAnswer: Positivism\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Post-Positivism”?\nA. Social Constructionism\nB. Post-Positivism\nC. Positivism\nD. Critical perspective\nAnswer: Post-Positivism\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Positivism”?\nA. Social Constructionism\nB. Positivism\nC. Post-Positivism\nD. Critical perspective\nAnswer: Positivism\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Post-Positivism”?\nA. Critical perspective\nB. Social Constructionism\nC. Post-Positivism\nD. Positivism\nAnswer: Post-Positivism\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Critical perspective”?\nA. Positivism\nB. Post-Positivism\nC. Social Constructionism\nD. Critical perspective\nAnswer: Critical perspective\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Positivism?\nA: empirical observation, measurement and regularities in phenomena\nQ: Key points for Positivism\nA: objectivity; measurement; empirical evidence\nQ: What is Post-Positivism?\nA: empirical observation, measurement and regularities in phenomena\nQ: Key points for Post-Positivism\nA: objectivity; measurement; empirical evidence\nQ: What is Critical perspective?\nA: Critical perspective is a focused concept within “Paradigms of Western Psychology: Positivism, Post-Positivism, Critical perspective, Social Constructionism, Existential Phenomenology, and Co-operative Enquiry”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Critical perspective\nA: definition and scope of Critical perspective; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Social Constructionism?\nA: how meanings and categories are produced through language, interaction and history\nQ: Key points for Social Constructionism\nA: language; social interaction; context",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Make a paradigm comparison chart. For each paradigm, learn its assumptions about reality/knowledge and its preferred approach to inquiry. The syllabus expects these six named paradigms.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nFoundations of Psychology: The book introduces psychology as the scientific study of behavior and mental life, then traces major historical schools and viewpoints. It presents the goals of scientific psychology—description, explanation, prediction and control—and discusses structuralism, functionalism, Gestalt psychology, psychoanalysis and behaviorism alongside biological, learning, cognitive, sociocultural and humanistic viewpoints. It also distinguishes major fields such as clinical, counseling, developmental, experimental and physiological psychology.",
