          "id": 1,
          "title": "Research: Meaning, Purpose and Dimensions",
          "explanation": "Define research as systematic inquiry and organize its purposes and dimensions before studying specific designs and methods.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nDefine research as systematic inquiry and organize its purposes and dimensions before studying specific designs and methods.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nMeaning of research • Purpose of research • Dimensions of research\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Meaning of research”?\nA. Dimensions of research\nB. None of the above\nC. Meaning of research\nD. Purpose of research\nAnswer: Meaning of research\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Purpose of research”?\nA. Meaning of research\nB. Dimensions of research\nC. None of the above\nD. Purpose of research\nAnswer: Purpose of research\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Meaning of research”?\nA. Purpose of research\nB. Dimensions of research\nC. None of the above\nD. Meaning of research\nAnswer: Meaning of research\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Purpose of research”?\nA. Purpose of research\nB. Meaning of research\nC. Dimensions of research\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Purpose of research\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Dimensions of research”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Dimensions of research\nC. Meaning of research\nD. Purpose of research\nAnswer: Dimensions of research\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 2: Research Methods in Psychology\nQ1. The point of view that knowledge is acquired by using the senses is called\na. rationalism\nb. voluntarism\nc. behaviorism\nd. empiricism\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 2: Research Methods in Psychology\nQ2. Which one of the following is not a step associated with the scientific method?\na. Reject all operational definitions\nb. Form a hypothesis\nc. Gather data\nd. Accept or reject the hypothesis\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 2: Research Methods in Psychology\nQ3. Sometimes a hypothesis is rejected that should be accepted. This is called\na. a Type I error\nb. an alpha error\nc. a Type II error\nd. an intrinsic error\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 2: Research Methods in Psychology\nQ4. Naturalistic observation requires a researcher to study behavior\na. in animals only\nb. as it is happening in its own setting\nc. using two independent variables\nd. by making sure the subjects know they are being observed\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 2: Research Methods in Psychology\nQ5. The behavior of Anna O. was studied with the assistance of what method?\na. The clinical method\nb. Naturalistic observation\nc. The experimental method\nd. The correlational method Research Methods in Psychology: Gathering Data 27\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 2: Research Methods in Psychology\nQ6. A population is\na. a very large sample\nb. defined by its bias\nc. a subset of a sample\nd. a well-defined group\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 2: Research Methods in Psychology\nQ7. The research of Lewis Terman on gifted children is an example of\na. the experimental method\nb. the clinical method\nc. the testing method\nd. the validity method\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 2: Research Methods in Psychology\nQ8. The size of the right foot can usually be used to predict the size of the left foot. This is an example of a\na. zero correlation\nb. negative correlation\nc. positive correlation\nd. lack of covariance\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 2: Research Methods in Psychology\nQ9. In an experiment, the control group\na. receives no treatment\nb. receives a novel treatment\nc. is expected to provide particularly interesting data\nd. is the error variance group\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 2: Research Methods in Psychology\nQ10. The variable that is assigned to the subjects by the experimenter is called\na. the dependent variable\nb. the independent variable\nc. the organismic variable\nd. the congruent variable\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Meaning of research?\nA: systematic, objective and empirical inquiry used to generate or test knowledge\nQ: Key points for Meaning of research\nA: systematic; objective; empirical; replicable where appropriate\nQ: What is Purpose of research?\nA: description, explanation, prediction and application/improvement\nQ: Key points for Purpose of research\nA: describe; explain; predict; apply\nQ: What is Dimensions of research?\nA: Dimensions of research is a focused concept within “Research: Meaning, Purpose, and Dimensions”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Dimensions of research\nA: definition and scope of Dimensions of research; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Define research as systematic inquiry and organize its purposes and dimensions before studying specific designs and methods.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nResearch Methods in Psychology: The book presents the scientific method as a systematic way of asking questions, forming hypotheses, collecting evidence and evaluating conclusions. It covers naturalistic observation, clinical and case-study methods, surveys, psychological testing, correlational research and experiments, including the roles of samples, populations, independent and dependent variables, control groups, random processes, reliability and validity. Correlation is treated as a relationship between variables rather than proof of causation.",
