          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Quantitative research”?\nA. Quantitative research\nB. Qualitative research\nC. Mixed methods approach\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Quantitative research\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Qualitative research”?\nA. Mixed methods approach\nB. None of the above\nC. Qualitative research\nD. Quantitative research\nAnswer: Qualitative research\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Mixed methods approach”?\nA. Quantitative research\nB. Qualitative research\nC. None of the above\nD. Mixed methods approach\nAnswer: Mixed methods approach\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Quantitative research?\nA: numerical measurement and statistical analysis of variables and relationships\nQ: Key points for Quantitative research\nA: measurement; numbers; statistical analysis\nQ: What is Qualitative research?\nA: study of meanings, experiences, processes and contexts using rich non-numerical data\nQ: Key points for Qualitative research\nA: interviews/observations; meaning; context; interpretive analysis\nQ: What is Mixed methods approach?\nA: intentional integration of quantitative and qualitative approaches\nQ: Key points for Mixed methods approach\nA: integration; complementarity; triangulation; sequential/concurrent designs",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Compare quantitative, qualitative and mixed-methods approaches by their aims, kinds of data, logic of inquiry and integration of evidence.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nResearch Methods in Psychology: The book presents the scientific method as a systematic way of asking questions, forming hypotheses, collecting evidence and evaluating conclusions. It covers naturalistic observation, clinical and case-study methods, surveys, psychological testing, correlational research and experiments, including the roles of samples, populations, independent and dependent variables, control groups, random processes, reliability and validity. Correlation is treated as a relationship between variables rather than proof of causation.",
