          "id": 4,
          "title": "Areas of Psychological Testing",
          "explanation": "Create an assessment-domain map: what each test area attempts to measure and where it is applied.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nCreate an assessment-domain map: what each test area attempts to measure and where it is applied.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nIntelligence tests • Creativity tests • Neuropsychological tests • Aptitude tests • Personality assessment • Interest inventories\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Intelligence tests”?\nA. Aptitude tests\nB. Intelligence tests\nC. Creativity tests\nD. Neuropsychological tests\nAnswer: Intelligence tests\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Creativity tests”?\nA. Creativity tests\nB. Intelligence tests\nC. Neuropsychological tests\nD. Aptitude tests\nAnswer: Creativity tests\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Intelligence tests”?\nA. Neuropsychological tests\nB. Aptitude tests\nC. Intelligence tests\nD. Creativity tests\nAnswer: Intelligence tests\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Creativity tests”?\nA. Aptitude tests\nB. Creativity tests\nC. Intelligence tests\nD. Neuropsychological tests\nAnswer: Creativity tests\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Neuropsychological tests”?\nA. Intelligence tests\nB. Creativity tests\nC. Aptitude tests\nD. Neuropsychological tests\nAnswer: Neuropsychological tests\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Intelligence tests?\nA: Intelligence tests is a focused concept within “Areas of testing: Intelligence, creativity, neuropsychological tests, aptitude, Personality assessment, interest inventories”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Intelligence tests\nA: definition and scope of Intelligence tests; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Creativity tests?\nA: Creativity tests is a focused concept within “Areas of testing: Intelligence, creativity, neuropsychological tests, aptitude, Personality assessment, interest inventories”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Creativity tests\nA: definition and scope of Creativity tests; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Neuropsychological tests?\nA: Neuropsychological tests is a focused concept within “Areas of testing: Intelligence, creativity, neuropsychological tests, aptitude, Personality assessment, interest inventories”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Neuropsychological tests\nA: definition and scope of Neuropsychological tests; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Aptitude tests?\nA: Aptitude tests is a focused concept within “Areas of testing: Intelligence, creativity, neuropsychological tests, aptitude, Personality assessment, interest inventories”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Aptitude tests\nA: definition and scope of Aptitude tests; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Create an assessment-domain map: what each test area attempts to measure and where it is applied.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nIntelligence: The intelligence chapter defines intelligence in terms of effective thinking and functioning and examines how it is measured. It covers the Stanford-Binet and Wechsler approaches, mental and chronological age, IQ, verbal and performance abilities, and the concepts of reliability and validity. It also discusses factor-analytic approaches, general and specific abilities, multiple intelligences, and the continuing nature-nurture issue in intellectual development.\nPersonality: The personality chapter defines personality as a distinctive pattern of traits and processes and compares type-trait, psychodynamic, neo-Freudian, learning and humanistic approaches. It covers Freud's id, ego and superego, Jung's archetypes and collective unconscious, Adlerian ideas, learning-based personality formation and Maslow's humanistic perspective. It also introduces personality assessment, including objective and projective approaches such as the Rorschach and MMPI.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
          "bookmarks": false,
