          "id": 4,
          "title": "Perceptual Organisation",
          "explanation": "Study how perceptual systems organize input, including figure-ground separation and Gestalt laws of organization.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nStudy how perceptual systems organize input, including figure-ground separation and Gestalt laws of organization.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nGestalt organization • Figure and ground • Laws of organization\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Gestalt organization”?\nA. Laws of organization\nB. None of the above\nC. Gestalt organization\nD. Figure and ground\nAnswer: Gestalt organization\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Figure and ground”?\nA. Gestalt organization\nB. Laws of organization\nC. None of the above\nD. Figure and ground\nAnswer: Figure and ground\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Gestalt organization”?\nA. Figure and ground\nB. Laws of organization\nC. None of the above\nD. Gestalt organization\nAnswer: Gestalt organization\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Figure and ground”?\nA. Figure and ground\nB. Gestalt organization\nC. Laws of organization\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Figure and ground\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Laws of organization”?\nA. Gestalt organization\nB. Figure and ground\nC. None of the above\nD. Laws of organization\nAnswer: Laws of organization\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 5: Perception\nQ1. According to Koffka, the actual world “out there,” the world as defined by physics is\na. the phenomenal world\nb. the geographical world\nc. the psychological world\nd. the subjective world\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 5: Perception\nQ2. The capacity to see a bird in the sky is an example of\na. the Ponzo illusion\nb. a cell assembly working\nc. a monocular cue\nd. figure-ground perception\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 5: Perception\nQ3. One of the following is not a Gestalt law.\na. Proximity\nb. Similarity\nc. The cognitive hypothesis\nd. Closure\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 5: Perception\nQ4. What hypothesis states that we not only perceive, but also know what we are perceiving?\na. The cognitive hypothesis\nb. The sensory hypothesis\nc. The motor-neuron hypothesis\nd. The Wertheimer-Koffka hypothesis\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 5: Perception\nQ5. An illusion is\na. a false belief\nb. a kind of hallucination\nc. the same thing as a delusion\nd. a false perception\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 5: Perception\nQ6. The vase-faces drawing is said to be ambiguous, meaning that\na. its borders are fuzzy\nb. it can be perceived in more than one way Perception: Why Do Things Look the Way They Do? 69\nc. it can be perceived in one way only\nd. it does not meet the criterion of subjectivity\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 5: Perception\nQ7. The Moon illusion\na. is caused by large changes in the Moon’s distance from the Earth\nb. provides a good example of size constancy\nc. provides a case in which size constancy breaks down\nd. violates figure-ground perception\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 5: Perception\nQ8. The principal depth perception cue associated with binocular vision is\na. linear perspective\nb. texture gradient\nc. motion parallax\nd. retinal disparity\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 5: Perception\nQ9. One of the following is not a kind of extrasensory perception.\na. Psychokinesis\nb. Precognition\nc. Telepathy\nd. Clairvoyance\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 5: Perception\nQ10. What is the status of psi powers in psychology as a science?\na. Psi powers are proven facts\nb. No one has done experiments on psi powers\nc. The reality of psi powers is still open to question\nd. Telepathy is real, but clairvoyance is not\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Gestalt organization?\nA: Gestalt organization is a focused concept within “Perceptual Organization: Gestalt, Figure and Ground, Law of Organization”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Gestalt organization\nA: definition and scope of Gestalt organization; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Figure and ground?\nA: Figure and ground is a focused concept within “Perceptual Organization: Gestalt, Figure and Ground, Law of Organization”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Figure and ground\nA: definition and scope of Figure and ground; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Laws of organization?\nA: Laws of organization is a focused concept within “Perceptual Organization: Gestalt, Figure and Ground, Law of Organization”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Laws of organization\nA: definition and scope of Laws of organization; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Study how perceptual systems organize input, including figure-ground separation and Gestalt laws of organization.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nPerception: The perception chapter examines how sensory information becomes an organized psychological experience. It covers the distinction between the geographical and psychological worlds, Gestalt organization and figure-ground perception, perceptual learning, constancies and illusions, and binocular and monocular depth cues. It also discusses movement perception and the status of unusual perceptual claims such as psi powers within scientific psychology.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
          "bookmarks": false,
          "review_date": "",
          "last_reviewed": "",
