          "title": "Perceptual Constancy and Illusions",
          "explanation": "Study constancies, illusions and the perception of form, depth and movement as distinct but related perceptual problems.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nStudy constancies, illusions and the perception of form, depth and movement as distinct but related perceptual problems.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nSize constancy • Shape constancy • Color constancy • Illusions • Perception of form • Perception of depth • Perception of movement\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Size constancy”?\nA. Color constancy\nB. Illusions\nC. Size constancy\nD. Shape constancy\nAnswer: Size constancy\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Shape constancy”?\nA. Shape constancy\nB. Size constancy\nC. Color constancy\nD. Illusions\nAnswer: Shape constancy\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Size constancy”?\nA. Shape constancy\nB. Color constancy\nC. Illusions\nD. Size constancy\nAnswer: Size constancy\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Shape constancy”?\nA. Illusions\nB. Shape constancy\nC. Size constancy\nD. Color constancy\nAnswer: Shape constancy\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Color constancy”?\nA. Size constancy\nB. Shape constancy\nC. Illusions\nD. Color constancy\nAnswer: Color constancy\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Size constancy?\nA: Size constancy is a focused concept within “Perceptual Constancy: Size, Shape, and Color; Illusions; Perception of Form, Depth and Movement”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Size constancy\nA: definition and scope of Size constancy; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Shape constancy?\nA: Shape constancy is a focused concept within “Perceptual Constancy: Size, Shape, and Color; Illusions; Perception of Form, Depth and Movement”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Shape constancy\nA: definition and scope of Shape constancy; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Color constancy?\nA: Color constancy is a focused concept within “Perceptual Constancy: Size, Shape, and Color; Illusions; Perception of Form, Depth and Movement”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Color constancy\nA: definition and scope of Color constancy; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Illusions?\nA: systematic misperceptions in which an external stimulus is present but interpreted differently\nQ: Key points for Illusions\nA: context effects; organization; depth cues",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Study constancies, illusions and the perception of form, depth and movement as distinct but related perceptual problems.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nPerception: The perception chapter examines how sensory information becomes an organized psychological experience. It covers the distinction between the geographical and psychological worlds, Gestalt organization and figure-ground perception, perceptual learning, constancies and illusions, and binocular and monocular depth cues. It also discusses movement perception and the status of unusual perceptual claims such as psi powers within scientific psychology.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
          "bookmarks": false,
          "review_date": "",
