          "title": "Motivation and Learning in Perception",
          "explanation": "Study how prior learning and motivational factors can influence perceptual interpretation.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nStudy how prior learning and motivational factors can influence perceptual interpretation.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nMotivation in perception • Learning in perception\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Motivation in perception”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Motivation in perception\nC. Learning in perception\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Motivation in perception\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Learning in perception”?\nA. Motivation in perception\nB. None of the above\nC. None of the above\nD. Learning in perception\nAnswer: Learning in perception\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Motivation in perception”?\nA. None of the above\nB. None of the above\nC. Motivation in perception\nD. Learning in perception\nAnswer: Motivation in perception\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Learning in perception”?\nA. Learning in perception\nB. Motivation in perception\nC. None of the above\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Learning in perception\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Motivation in perception?\nA: Motivation in perception is a focused concept within “Role of motivation and learning in perception”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Motivation in perception\nA: definition and scope of Motivation in perception; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Learning in perception?\nA: Learning in perception is a focused concept within “Role of motivation and learning in perception”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Learning in perception\nA: definition and scope of Learning in perception; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Study how prior learning and motivational factors can influence perceptual interpretation.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nPerception: The perception chapter examines how sensory information becomes an organized psychological experience. It covers the distinction between the geographical and psychological worlds, Gestalt organization and figure-ground perception, perceptual learning, constancies and illusions, and binocular and monocular depth cues. It also discusses movement perception and the status of unusual perceptual claims such as psi powers within scientific psychology.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
          "bookmarks": false,
          "review_date": "",
