          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nCompare the major memory stages/systems and distinguish episodic, semantic and procedural memory.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nSensory memory • Short-term memory • Working memory • Long-term memory • Declarative memory • Episodic memory • Semantic memory • Procedural memory\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Sensory memory”?\nA. Short-term memory\nB. Working memory\nC. Long-term memory\nD. Sensory memory\nAnswer: Sensory memory\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Short-term memory”?\nA. Long-term memory\nB. Short-term memory\nC. Sensory memory\nD. Working memory\nAnswer: Short-term memory\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Sensory memory”?\nA. Sensory memory\nB. Short-term memory\nC. Working memory\nD. Long-term memory\nAnswer: Sensory memory\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Short-term memory”?\nA. Working memory\nB. Long-term memory\nC. Short-term memory\nD. Sensory memory\nAnswer: Short-term memory\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which is a component of Baddeley and Hitch's working memory model?\nA. Unconditioned stimulus\nB. Primary mental ability\nC. Locus of control\nD. Phonological loop\nAnswer: Phonological loop\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Sensory memory?\nA: Sensory memory is a focused concept within “Stages of memory: Sensory memory, Short-term memory (Working memory), Long-term Memory (Declarative – Episodic and Semantic; Procedural)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Sensory memory\nA: definition and scope of Sensory memory; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Short-term memory?\nA: Short-term memory is a focused concept within “Stages of memory: Sensory memory, Short-term memory (Working memory), Long-term Memory (Declarative – Episodic and Semantic; Procedural)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Short-term memory\nA: definition and scope of Short-term memory; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Working memory?\nA: limited system for temporarily holding and manipulating information\nQ: Key points for Working memory\nA: central executive; phonological loop; visuospatial sketchpad; episodic buffer\nQ: What is Long-term memory?\nA: Long-term memory is a focused concept within “Stages of memory: Sensory memory, Short-term memory (Working memory), Long-term Memory (Declarative – Episodic and Semantic; Procedural)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Long-term memory\nA: definition and scope of Long-term memory; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Compare the major memory stages/systems and distinguish episodic, semantic and procedural memory.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nLearning: The learning chapter describes learning as a relatively enduring change in behavior or behavioral potential associated with experience. It covers classical conditioning, including conditioned and unconditioned stimuli and responses, extinction, discrimination and generalization; operant conditioning and reinforcement; Thorndike's law of effect; observational learning and vicarious reinforcement; insight and learning sets; and the relationship between learning and memory. Memory processes include encoding, storage and retrieval.",
