          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. If new learning disrupts old learning, it is called:\nA. Repression\nB. Proactive interference\nC. Retroactive interference\nD. Decay\nAnswer: Retroactive interference\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Retrieval failure”?\nA. Motivated forgetting\nB. Retrieval failure\nC. Interference theory\nD. Decay\nAnswer: Retrieval failure\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. If new learning disrupts old learning, it is called:\nA. Decay\nB. Repression\nC. Proactive interference\nD. Retroactive interference\nAnswer: Retroactive interference\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Retrieval failure”?\nA. Decay\nB. Motivated forgetting\nC. Retrieval failure\nD. Interference theory\nAnswer: Retrieval failure\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Decay”?\nA. Retrieval failure\nB. Motivated forgetting\nC. Decay\nD. Interference theory\nAnswer: Decay\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Interference theory?\nA: forgetting through competition between memories\nQ: Key points for Interference theory\nA: proactive: old → new; retroactive: new → old\nQ: What is Retrieval failure?\nA: Retrieval failure is a focused concept within “Theories of Forgetting: Interference, Retrieval Failure, Decay, Motivated forgetting”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Retrieval failure\nA: definition and scope of Retrieval failure; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Decay?\nA: Decay is a focused concept within “Theories of Forgetting: Interference, Retrieval Failure, Decay, Motivated forgetting”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Decay\nA: definition and scope of Decay; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Motivated forgetting?\nA: Motivated forgetting is a focused concept within “Theories of Forgetting: Interference, Retrieval Failure, Decay, Motivated forgetting”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Motivated forgetting\nA: definition and scope of Motivated forgetting; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Compare the four named explanations for forgetting and identify the mechanism emphasized by each.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nLearning: The learning chapter describes learning as a relatively enduring change in behavior or behavioral potential associated with experience. It covers classical conditioning, including conditioned and unconditioned stimuli and responses, extinction, discrimination and generalization; operant conditioning and reinforcement; Thorndike's law of effect; observational learning and vicarious reinforcement; insight and learning sets; and the relationship between learning and memory. Memory processes include encoding, storage and retrieval.",
