          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Associationism”?\nA. Feature integration model\nB. Associationism\nC. Gestalt perspective\nD. Information processing perspective\nAnswer: Associationism\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Gestalt perspective”?\nA. Feature integration model\nB. Gestalt perspective\nC. Associationism\nD. Information processing perspective\nAnswer: Gestalt perspective\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Associationism”?\nA. Information processing perspective\nB. Feature integration model\nC. Associationism\nD. Gestalt perspective\nAnswer: Associationism\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Gestalt perspective”?\nA. Information processing perspective\nB. Feature integration model\nC. Gestalt perspective\nD. Associationism\nAnswer: Gestalt perspective\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Information processing perspective”?\nA. Associationism\nB. Gestalt perspective\nC. Feature integration model\nD. Information processing perspective\nAnswer: Information processing perspective\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Associationism?\nA: Associationism is a focused concept within “Theoretical perspectives on thought processes: Associationism, Gestalt, Information processing, Feature integration model”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Associationism\nA: definition and scope of Associationism; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Gestalt perspective?\nA: Gestalt perspective is a focused concept within “Theoretical perspectives on thought processes: Associationism, Gestalt, Information processing, Feature integration model”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Gestalt perspective\nA: definition and scope of Gestalt perspective; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Information processing perspective?\nA: Information processing perspective is a focused concept within “Theoretical perspectives on thought processes: Associationism, Gestalt, Information processing, Feature integration model”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Information processing perspective\nA: definition and scope of Information processing perspective; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Feature integration model?\nA: Feature integration model is a focused concept within “Theoretical perspectives on thought processes: Associationism, Gestalt, Information processing, Feature integration model”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Feature integration model\nA: definition and scope of Feature integration model; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Compare the four perspectives by asking how each explains thought, organization and processing of information.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nThinking: The thinking chapter examines concepts, reasoning, problem solving, decision making and creativity. It describes concepts as mental organizations of experience and distinguishes different forms of reasoning. Problem solving is discussed in terms of strategies, mental sets and obstacles such as functional fixedness. The chapter also contrasts logical thinking with common errors and identifies divergent thinking as central to creative production.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
