              "id": 3,
              "title": "Information processing perspective",
              "content_notes": "The information-processing perspective explains thought as a set of processes through which information is received, represented, stored, transformed and used. The uploaded material links this perspective with attention, memory, problem solving and decision making.\n\nKEY POINTS\n• Mental activity is described in terms of information processing.\n• Attention selects or prioritizes information.\n• Memory provides stored representations used in thinking.\n• Problem solving and decision making involve multiple processing operations.\n\nDISTINCTION / CAUTION\nInformation processing is a framework for describing cognitive operations; it is not one single experiment or one single cognitive ability.",
