          "title": "Problem Solving and Decision Making",
          "explanation": "Separate problem solving from decision making, then compare types, strategies, obstacles and models.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nSeparate problem solving from decision making, then compare types, strategies, obstacles and models.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nTypes of problem solving • Problem-solving strategies • Obstacles to problem solving • Types of decision-making • Decision-making models\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Types of problem solving”?\nA. Obstacles to problem solving\nB. Types of decision-making\nC. Types of problem solving\nD. Problem-solving strategies\nAnswer: Types of problem solving\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Problem-solving strategies”?\nA. Problem-solving strategies\nB. Types of problem solving\nC. Obstacles to problem solving\nD. Types of decision-making\nAnswer: Problem-solving strategies\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Types of problem solving”?\nA. Problem-solving strategies\nB. Obstacles to problem solving\nC. Types of decision-making\nD. Types of problem solving\nAnswer: Types of problem solving\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Problem-solving strategies”?\nA. Types of decision-making\nB. Problem-solving strategies\nC. Types of problem solving\nD. Obstacles to problem solving\nAnswer: Problem-solving strategies\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Obstacles to problem solving”?\nA. Problem-solving strategies\nB. Types of decision-making\nC. Obstacles to problem solving\nD. Types of problem solving\nAnswer: Obstacles to problem solving\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 9: Thinking\nQ1. The process of thinking about thinking is called\na. cognitive existentialism\nb. symbolic production\nc. functional reflection\nd. metathought\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 9: Thinking\nQ2. What kind of a concept strings together perceived attributes?\na. A relational concept\nb. A disjunctive concept\nc. An iconic concept\nd. A conjunctive concept\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 9: Thinking\nQ3. Which of the following identifies an object or an idea that fits a concept, that can be contained within it?\na. Positive exemplar\nb. Negative exemplar\nc. Bipolar exemplar\nd. Transformational exemplar\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 9: Thinking\nQ4. Step-by-step instructions for operating a microwave oven provide an example of\na. a heuristic approach\nb. a means-end analysis\nc. an algorithm\nd. an insight analysis\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 9: Thinking\nQ5. Let’s say you figure out the square root of 12 without a formula. Instead, you rely on your understanding of the concept of a square root. What kind of a problem-solving approach are you using?\na. An algorithm\nb. A heuristic approach\nc. A means-end analysis\nd. An operant reflection\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 9: Thinking\nQ6. Which of the following correctly defines a mental set?\na. A conscious conditioned reflex\nb. An unconscious wish\nc. A subconscious determining tendency\nd. A false negative\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 9: Thinking\nQ7. What exists when there is a need to use a tool or familiar object in a novel way and one can’t perceive the novel way?\na. Cognitive slippage\nb. Mental facilitation\nc. Functional fixedness\nd. Transformational perception\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 9: Thinking\nQ8. What kind of reasoning is characterized by making observations and gathering information until a general conclusion is reached?\na. Inductive reasoning\nb. Deductive reasoning\nc. If-then reasoning\nd. Relational reasoning\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 9: Thinking\nQ9. Deductive reasoning is reasoning in which\na. a premise follows from a conclusion\nb. a premise follows from a hyperpremise\nc. a conclusion follows from a metaconclusion\nd. a conclusion follows from a premise\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 9: Thinking\nQ10. What is the core feature of the creative process?\na. Convergent thinking\nb. Divergent thinking\nc. Congruent thinking\nd. Reliable facts Thinking: Exploring Mental Life 135\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).",
