          "title": "Metacognition",
          "explanation": "Distinguish knowing about one's cognition from regulating and monitoring one's cognitive processes.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nDistinguish knowing about one's cognition from regulating and monitoring one's cognitive processes.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nMetacognitive knowledge • Metacognitive regulation\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Metacognitive knowledge”?\nA. Metacognitive knowledge\nB. Metacognitive regulation\nC. None of the above\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Metacognitive knowledge\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Metacognitive regulation”?\nA. None of the above\nB. None of the above\nC. Metacognitive regulation\nD. Metacognitive knowledge\nAnswer: Metacognitive regulation\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Metacognitive knowledge”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Metacognitive knowledge\nC. Metacognitive regulation\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Metacognitive knowledge\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Metacognitive regulation”?\nA. Metacognitive knowledge\nB. None of the above\nC. None of the above\nD. Metacognitive regulation\nAnswer: Metacognitive regulation\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Metacognitive knowledge?\nA: Metacognitive knowledge is a focused concept within “Metacognition: Metacognitive knowledge and Metacognitive regulation”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Metacognitive knowledge\nA: definition and scope of Metacognitive knowledge; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Metacognitive regulation?\nA: Metacognitive regulation is a focused concept within “Metacognition: Metacognitive knowledge and Metacognitive regulation”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Metacognitive regulation\nA: definition and scope of Metacognitive regulation; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Distinguish knowing about one's cognition from regulating and monitoring one's cognitive processes.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nThinking: The thinking chapter examines concepts, reasoning, problem solving, decision making and creativity. It describes concepts as mental organizations of experience and distinguishes different forms of reasoning. Problem solving is discussed in terms of strategies, mental sets and obstacles such as functional fixedness. The chapter also contrasts logical thinking with common errors and identifies divergent thinking as central to creative production.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
          "bookmarks": false,
          "review_date": "",
