          "title": "Other Personality Theories",
          "explanation": "Study these as three distinct frameworks: locus of control, explanatory style and moral development.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nStudy these as three distinct frameworks: locus of control, explanatory style and moral development.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nRotter's Locus of Control • Seligman's Explanatory styles • Kohlberg’s theory of Moral development\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Rotter's Locus of Control”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Rotter's Locus of Control\nC. Seligman's Explanatory styles\nD. Kohlberg’s theory of Moral development\nAnswer: Rotter's Locus of Control\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Seligman's Explanatory styles”?\nA. Kohlberg’s theory of Moral development\nB. None of the above\nC. Seligman's Explanatory styles\nD. Rotter's Locus of Control\nAnswer: Seligman's Explanatory styles\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Rotter's Locus of Control”?\nA. Kohlberg’s theory of Moral development\nB. None of the above\nC. Rotter's Locus of Control\nD. Seligman's Explanatory styles\nAnswer: Rotter's Locus of Control\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Seligman's Explanatory styles”?\nA. Rotter's Locus of Control\nB. Kohlberg’s theory of Moral development\nC. None of the above\nD. Seligman's Explanatory styles\nAnswer: Seligman's Explanatory styles\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Kohlberg’s theory of Moral development”?\nA. Kohlberg’s theory of Moral development\nB. Rotter's Locus of Control\nC. Seligman's Explanatory styles\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Kohlberg’s theory of Moral development\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Rotter's Locus of Control?\nA: expectancy that outcomes are controlled internally or externally\nQ: Key points for Rotter's Locus of Control\nA: internal locus; external locus; expectancy\nQ: What is Seligman's Explanatory styles?\nA: Seligman's Explanatory styles is a focused concept within “Other theories: Rotter's Locus of Control, Seligman's Explanatory styles, Kohlberg’s theory of Moral development”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Seligman's Explanatory styles\nA: definition and scope of Seligman's Explanatory styles; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Kohlberg’s theory of Moral development?\nA: stages of moral reasoning across pre-conventional, conventional and post-conventional levels\nQ: Key points for Kohlberg’s theory of Moral development\nA: three levels; moral reasoning; stage sequence",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Study these as three distinct frameworks: locus of control, explanatory style and moral development.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nPersonality: The personality chapter defines personality as a distinctive pattern of traits and processes and compares type-trait, psychodynamic, neo-Freudian, learning and humanistic approaches. It covers Freud's id, ego and superego, Jung's archetypes and collective unconscious, Adlerian ideas, learning-based personality formation and Maslow's humanistic perspective. It also introduces personality assessment, including objective and projective approaches such as the Rorschach and MMPI.",
