          "flashcards": "Q: What is Concept of stress and coping?\nA: stress as appraisal of demands relative to resources, with coping efforts to manage demands/emotions\nQ: Key points for Concept of stress and coping\nA: stressor; appraisal; problem-focused; emotion-focused\nQ: What is Models of stress and coping?\nA: Models of stress and coping is a focused concept within “Stress and Coping: Concept, Models, Type A, B, C, D behaviors”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Models of stress and coping\nA: definition and scope of Models of stress and coping; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Type A behavior?\nA: Type A behavior is a focused concept within “Stress and Coping: Concept, Models, Type A, B, C, D behaviors”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Type A behavior\nA: definition and scope of Type A behavior; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Type B behavior?\nA: Type B behavior is a focused concept within “Stress and Coping: Concept, Models, Type A, B, C, D behaviors”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Type B behavior\nA: definition and scope of Type B behavior; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nEmotions: The emotions chapter treats emotions as experiences that include motivational, physiological and psychological dimensions. It examines basic dimensions of emotion, major theories including James-Lange and competing approaches, and the relationship between emotion, action and cognition. It also covers stress and health, including chronic stress and Type A behavior, and explains approach-approach, approach-avoidance, double approach-avoidance and avoidance-avoidance conflicts.",
