          "id": 4,
          "title": "Groups and Social Influence",
          "explanation": "Build a clear understanding of Groups and Social Influence, then connect its key ideas, distinctions, and applications.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nBuild a clear understanding of Groups and Social Influence, then connect its key ideas, distinctions, and applications.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nSocial Facilitation • Social loafing • Conformity • Peer Pressure • Persuasion • Compliance • Obedience • Social Power • Reactance\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Social Facilitation”?\nA. Peer Pressure\nB. Social Facilitation\nC. Social loafing\nD. Conformity\nAnswer: Social Facilitation\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Social loafing”?\nA. Social loafing\nB. Social Facilitation\nC. Conformity\nD. Peer Pressure\nAnswer: Social loafing\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Social Facilitation”?\nA. Conformity\nB. Peer Pressure\nC. Social Facilitation\nD. Social loafing\nAnswer: Social Facilitation\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Social loafing”?\nA. Peer Pressure\nB. Social loafing\nC. Social Facilitation\nD. Conformity\nAnswer: Social loafing\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Asch's line-judgment studies are classically associated with:\nA. Attachment\nB. Leadership\nC. Conformity\nD. Obedience\nAnswer: Conformity\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Social Facilitation?\nA: Social Facilitation is a focused concept within “Group and Social influence (Social Facilitation; Social loafing); Social influence (Conformity, Peer Pressure, Persuasion, Compliance, Obedience, Social Power, Reactance)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Social Facilitation\nA: definition and scope of Social Facilitation; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Social loafing?\nA: Social loafing is a focused concept within “Group and Social influence (Social Facilitation; Social loafing); Social influence (Conformity, Peer Pressure, Persuasion, Compliance, Obedience, Social Power, Reactance)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Social loafing\nA: definition and scope of Social loafing; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Conformity?\nA: adjustment of behavior or judgment toward group norms\nQ: Key points for Conformity\nA: Asch; normative influence; informational influence\nQ: What is Peer Pressure?\nA: Peer Pressure is a focused concept within “Group and Social influence (Social Facilitation; Social loafing); Social influence (Conformity, Peer Pressure, Persuasion, Compliance, Obedience, Social Power, Reactance)”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Peer Pressure\nA: definition and scope of Peer Pressure; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nSocial Psychology: The social psychology chapter studies how interactions with other people influence thoughts, feelings and actions. It covers interpersonal attraction, attitudes and persuasion, social perception and attribution, conformity and social influence, cognitive dissonance, prosocial behavior, aggression, group processes and leadership. The chapter also introduces social influence experiments and the role of situational and interpersonal factors in behavior.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
          "bookmarks": false,
          "review_date": "",
