              "title": "Minimal Group Experiment",
              "content_notes": "The minimal-group paradigm demonstrates that people can show in-group favoritism even when group membership is created using minimal or relatively arbitrary distinctions. The uploaded NET material lists the Minimal Group Experiment with Social Identity Theory as a major intergroup-relations topic.\n\nKEY POINTS\n• Minimal categorization can create meaningful group distinctions.\n• In-group favoritism can emerge with minimal group formation.\n• The paradigm supports the importance of social categorization.\n• It is relevant to Social Identity Theory.\n\nDISTINCTION / CAUTION\nMinimal-group findings do not require a history of real conflict between groups; the point is that categorization itself can matter.",
