          "flashcards": "Q: What is Health applications?\nA: Health applications is a focused concept within “Applied social psychology: Health, Environment and Law; Personal space, crowding, and territoriality”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Health applications\nA: definition and scope of Health applications; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Environmental applications?\nA: Environmental applications is a focused concept within “Applied social psychology: Health, Environment and Law; Personal space, crowding, and territoriality”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Environmental applications\nA: definition and scope of Environmental applications; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Law applications?\nA: Law applications is a focused concept within “Applied social psychology: Health, Environment and Law; Personal space, crowding, and territoriality”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Law applications\nA: definition and scope of Law applications; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Personal space?\nA: Personal space is a focused concept within “Applied social psychology: Health, Environment and Law; Personal space, crowding, and territoriality”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Personal space\nA: definition and scope of Personal space; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Connect social-psychological principles with health, environmental and legal contexts, and study personal space, crowding and territoriality.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nSocial Psychology: The social psychology chapter studies how interactions with other people influence thoughts, feelings and actions. It covers interpersonal attraction, attitudes and persuasion, social perception and attribution, conformity and social influence, cognitive dissonance, prosocial behavior, aggression, group processes and leadership. The chapter also introduces social influence experiments and the role of situational and interpersonal factors in behavior.",
