          "explanation": "Study development through nature, principles, factors and stages. Keep biological and environmental influences conceptually distinct but interactive.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nStudy development through nature, principles, factors and stages. Keep biological and environmental influences conceptually distinct but interactive.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nNature of development • Principles of development • Factors in development • Stages of development\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Nature of development”?\nA. Nature of development\nB. Principles of development\nC. Factors in development\nD. Stages of development\nAnswer: Nature of development\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Development from head to toe is called:\nA. Lateral\nB. Cephalocaudal\nC. Proximodistal\nD. Reciprocal\nAnswer: Cephalocaudal\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Nature of development”?\nA. Stages of development\nB. Nature of development\nC. Principles of development\nD. Factors in development\nAnswer: Nature of development\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Development from head to toe is called:\nA. Reciprocal\nB. Lateral\nC. Cephalocaudal\nD. Proximodistal\nAnswer: Cephalocaudal\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Factors in development”?\nA. Factors in development\nB. Nature of development\nC. Principles of development\nD. Stages of development\nAnswer: Factors in development\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Nature of development?\nA: systematic change across the lifespan in biological, cognitive, emotional and social domains\nQ: Key points for Nature of development\nA: lifespan; multidimensionality; plasticity\nQ: What is Principles of development?\nA: organized patterns such as cephalocaudal and proximodistal development alongside individual differences\nQ: Key points for Principles of development\nA: cephalocaudal: head → toe; proximodistal: centre → outward; individual differences\nQ: What is Factors in development?\nA: Factors in development is a focused concept within “Developmental processes: Nature, Principles, Factors in development, Stages of Development”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Factors in development\nA: definition and scope of Factors in development; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Stages of development?\nA: Stages of development is a focused concept within “Developmental processes: Nature, Principles, Factors in development, Stages of Development”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Stages of development\nA: definition and scope of Stages of development; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Study development through nature, principles, factors and stages. Keep biological and environmental influences conceptually distinct but interactive.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nDevelopmental Psychology: The developmental chapter follows development from biological beginnings through childhood and later life. It covers reproductive and fetal development, Freud's psychosexual theory, Erikson's psychosocial stages, Piaget's cognitive stages and Kohlberg's moral-development levels. It also examines parental styles and the role of heredity and environment, showing how biological maturation and experience interact in development.",
