          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nCompare psychoanalytic, behavioristic and cognitive explanations of development.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nPsychoanalytical theories • Behavioristic theories • Cognitive theories\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Psychoanalytical theories”?\nA. Psychoanalytical theories\nB. Behavioristic theories\nC. Cognitive theories\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Psychoanalytical theories\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Behavioristic theories”?\nA. Behavioristic theories\nB. Psychoanalytical theories\nC. Cognitive theories\nD. None of the above\nAnswer: Behavioristic theories\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Psychoanalytical theories”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Psychoanalytical theories\nC. Behavioristic theories\nD. Cognitive theories\nAnswer: Psychoanalytical theories\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Which option is most directly associated with “Behavioristic theories”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Behavioristic theories\nC. Psychoanalytical theories\nD. Cognitive theories\nAnswer: Behavioristic theories\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Which option is most directly associated with “Cognitive theories”?\nA. None of the above\nB. Cognitive theories\nC. Psychoanalytical theories\nD. Behavioristic theories\nAnswer: Cognitive theories\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 11: Developmental Psychology\nQ1. The basic unit of heredity is the\na. chromosome\nb. gene\nc. trisomy 21 pattern\nd. ribonucleic acid (RNA) anomaly\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 11: Developmental Psychology\nQ2. From seven weeks to birth, the new being is called\na. a fetus\nb. an embryo\nc. a zygote\nd. a neonate\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 11: Developmental Psychology\nQ3. According to Freud’s usage, psychosexual energy is referred to as\na. libido\nb. erotic ambivalence\nc. metabolism\nd. genital potency\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 11: Developmental Psychology\nQ4. The Oedipus complex is associated with what psychosexual stage?\na. The oral stage\nb. The anal stage\nc. The genital stage\nd. The latency stage\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 11: Developmental Psychology\nQ5. A toddler with a particular positive psychosocial trait will be interested in exploring the immediate world and display an interest in novel stimulation. What is this trait?\na. Autonomy\nb. Identity\nc. Intimacy\nd. Generativity\nSource answer: A\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 11: Developmental Psychology\nQ6. An older person with a particular positive psychosocial trait can face approaching death with a certain amount of acceptance. What is this trait?\na. Generativity\nb. Isolation\nc. Identity\nd. Integrity\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 11: Developmental Psychology\nQ7. What method did Piaget use to study the child’s mind?\na. The experimental method\nb. The survey method\nc. The phenomenological method\nd. The correlational method\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 11: Developmental Psychology\nQ8. Magical thinking, anthropomorphic thinking, and egocentrism are associated with what stage of cognitive development?\na. Trust versus mistrust\nb. The sensorimotor stage\nc. The formal operations stage\nd. The preoperational stage\nSource answer: D\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 11: Developmental Psychology\nQ9. What level of moral development is associated with a law and order orientation?\na. The premoral level\nb. The preconventional level\nc. The conventional level\nd. The principled level\nSource answer: C\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).\n\nATTACHED BOOK SELF-TEST — Chapter 11: Developmental Psychology\nQ10. Research suggests that a parent who manifests what style tends to induce optimal social behaviors in the child?\na. Authoritarian-accepting\nb. Democratic-accepting\nc. Permissive-accepting\nd. Authoritarian-rejecting Developmental Psychology: How Children Become Adults 171\nSource answer: B\nSource: Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide (attached PDF).",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Psychoanalytical theories?\nA: Psychoanalytical theories is a focused concept within “Theories of development: Psychoanalytical, Behavioristic, and Cognitive”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Psychoanalytical theories\nA: definition and scope of Psychoanalytical theories; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Behavioristic theories?\nA: Behavioristic theories is a focused concept within “Theories of development: Psychoanalytical, Behavioristic, and Cognitive”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Behavioristic theories\nA: definition and scope of Behavioristic theories; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Cognitive theories?\nA: Cognitive theories is a focused concept within “Theories of development: Psychoanalytical, Behavioristic, and Cognitive”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Cognitive theories\nA: definition and scope of Cognitive theories; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
