          "title": "Wellbeing and Self-Growth",
          "explanation": "Compare hedonic and eudemonic wellbeing, then connect wellbeing with character strengths, resilience and post-traumatic growth.",
          "notes": "ACADEMIC CORE\nCompare hedonic and eudemonic wellbeing, then connect wellbeing with character strengths, resilience and post-traumatic growth.\n\nMICRO-TOPIC MAP\nTypes of wellbeing • Hedonic wellbeing • Eudemonic wellbeing • Character strengths • Resilience • Post-Traumatic Growth\n\nSOURCE BASIS\nThe topic is retained within the uploaded NET syllabus/material framework. Use the listed source books/notes below for the detailed treatment; the website should not substitute generic explanations where the source is silent.\n\nEXAM USE\nLearn the definitions, named theorists/models, classifications, mechanisms, distinctions and source-specific examples actually present in the material.",
          "status": "Not started",
          "pyqs": "PYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 1\nQ. Which option is most directly associated with “Types of wellbeing”?\nA. Types of wellbeing\nB. Hedonic wellbeing\nC. Eudemonic wellbeing\nD. Character strengths\nAnswer: Types of wellbeing\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.\n\nPYQ-STYLE PRACTICE 2\nQ. Hedonic wellbeing primarily emphasizes:\nA. Meaning and self-realization only\nB. Group conformity\nC. Cognitive restructuring\nD. Pleasure, positive affect and life satisfaction\nAnswer: Pleasure, positive affect and life satisfaction\nNote: Generated from the source framework; this is PYQ-style practice, not a verified past-year question.",
          "mcqs": "Q1. Which option is most directly associated with “Types of wellbeing”?\nA. Character strengths\nB. Types of wellbeing\nC. Hedonic wellbeing\nD. Eudemonic wellbeing\nAnswer: Types of wellbeing\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ2. Hedonic wellbeing primarily emphasizes:\nA. Pleasure, positive affect and life satisfaction\nB. Meaning and self-realization only\nC. Group conformity\nD. Cognitive restructuring\nAnswer: Pleasure, positive affect and life satisfaction\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.\n\nQ3. Eudemonic wellbeing emphasizes:\nA. Obedience\nB. Sensory thresholds\nC. Meaning, purpose and flourishing\nD. Only short-term pleasure\nAnswer: Meaning, purpose and flourishing\nExplanation: Review the micro-topic note for the defining feature, key distinction and application.",
          "flashcards": "Q: What is Types of wellbeing?\nA: Types of wellbeing is a focused concept within “Wellbeing and self-growth: Types of wellbeing (Hedonic and Eudemonic), Character strengths, Resilience and Post-Traumatic Growth”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Types of wellbeing\nA: definition and scope of Types of wellbeing; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application\nQ: What is Hedonic wellbeing?\nA: wellbeing emphasizing pleasure, positive affect and life satisfaction\nQ: Key points for Hedonic wellbeing\nA: positive affect; low negative affect; life satisfaction\nQ: What is Eudemonic wellbeing?\nA: wellbeing emphasizing meaning, purpose, growth and realizing capacities\nQ: Key points for Eudemonic wellbeing\nA: meaning; purpose; growth; self-realization\nQ: What is Character strengths?\nA: Character strengths is a focused concept within “Wellbeing and self-growth: Types of wellbeing (Hedonic and Eudemonic), Character strengths, Resilience and Post-Traumatic Growth”. Prepare it as an exam-sized learning node: define it, identify its key features, distinguish it from a close concept, and apply it to one short example.\nQ: Key points for Character strengths\nA: definition and scope of Character strengths; key features/components/stages as applicable; one comparison or common confusion; one example or application",
          "references": "",
          "detailed_notes": "Compare hedonic and eudemonic wellbeing, then connect wellbeing with character strengths, resilience and post-traumatic growth.\n\nATTACHED BOOK CONNECTION\nMotivation: The motivation chapter treats motives as forces behind action and explains their interaction with learning. It distinguishes biological drives from general and acquired motives, discusses unconscious motivation, and examines needs such as autonomy, achievement and related motives. The chapter also introduces Maslow's self-actualization and Frankl's emphasis on meaning, linking motivation with broader questions about purpose and human potential.\nEmotions: The emotions chapter treats emotions as experiences that include motivational, physiological and psychological dimensions. It examines basic dimensions of emotion, major theories including James-Lange and competing approaches, and the relationship between emotion, action and cognition. It also covers stress and health, including chronic stress and Type A behavior, and explains approach-approach, approach-avoidance, double approach-avoidance and avoidance-avoidance conflicts.",
          "source_scope": "Authoritative UGC NET Psychology syllabus + Prompts for notes.docx + Psychology: A Self-Teaching Guide. Generated practice is PYQ-style; attached-book self-test items are source-based.",
          "bookmarks": false,
